2. Requirements

BeamMP Server runs on Windows and Linux. For a public Internet server you should have a reachable IPv4 address, the selected BeamMP port open for both TCP and UDP, a BeamMP AuthKey, and enough host/network capacity for the map, vehicles, players, and mods you intend to run.

The normal default BeamMP port is:

30814

Both protocols are required:

TCP 30814
UDP 30814

If you host several BeamMP instances on one machine, assign a unique port to each instance, for example 30814, 30815, 30816.

For the weekly website webhook you also need Python 3, outbound HTTPS access, a website endpoint, and a private webhook secret shared only by the game server and website.

3. Create a BeamMP AuthKey

Use the official BeamMP Keymaster and sign in with Discord. Create a key, give it an internal name, copy the generated AuthKey, and store it securely.

Treat the AuthKey like a password. Do not post it to GitHub, screenshots, Discord, documentation, or public configuration examples. A single key should not be used by multiple simultaneously running servers.

4. Windows BeamMP installation

Create a dedicated directory such as:

C:\BeamMP-Server\

Install the Microsoft Visual C++ Redistributable required by BeamMP, then download the current official BeamMP Server executable and place it in that folder:

C:\BeamMP-Server\BeamMP-Server.exe

Run BeamMP-Server.exe once. The first startup creates files including:

ServerConfig.toml
Server.log
Resources\

Stop the server after those files are created so you can configure it.

5. Linux BeamMP installation

A dedicated location and service account are recommended. This guide uses:

/opt/beammp/

Create a dedicated service user:

sudo useradd \
  --system \
  --create-home \
  --home-dir /opt/beammp \
  --shell /usr/sbin/nologin \
  beammpserver

Download the correct current BeamMP Server build for your distribution and architecture. Put it in /opt/beammp/. For simpler service files, rename it to:

BeamMP-Server

Make it executable and set ownership:

sudo chmod +x /opt/beammp/BeamMP-Server
sudo chown -R beammpserver:beammpserver /opt/beammp

Start it once as the dedicated account:

sudo -u beammpserver \
  bash -c 'cd /opt/beammp && ./BeamMP-Server'

Allow the server to create ServerConfig.toml, Server.log, and Resources/, then stop it.

The BeamMP working directory matters because configuration, logs, and Resources are created/read relative to the process working directory.

6. Configure ServerConfig.toml

Open the generated ServerConfig.toml. A practical starting point is:

[General]
Port = 30814
AuthKey = "YOUR_PRIVATE_BEAMMP_AUTH_KEY"
AllowGuests = false
LogChat = false
Debug = false
IP = "::"
Private = false
InformationPacket = true
Name = "My BeamMP Server"
Tags = "Racing,Modded,Community"
MaxCars = 2
MaxPlayers = 20
Map = "/levels/gridmap_v2/info.json"
Description = "Community BeamMP server"
ResourceFolder = "Resources"

Important settings:

Port: must match your firewall, router forwarding, and direct-connect port.

AuthKey: paste your private Keymaster value here.

Private = false: makes the server eligible for the public list.

Private = true: use for a private/direct-connect server.

MaxPlayers: choose according to hardware, mods, vehicles, and network capacity.

MaxCars: more vehicles can substantially increase load.

Map: BeamNG level path ending in /info.json.

ResourceFolder: normally leave as Resources.

7. Network setup

Home hosting

If users outside your home network will join, forward the selected BeamMP port from the router to the server's LAN IPv4 address.

Default rules:

External TCP 30814 -> Server IPv4 TCP 30814
External UDP 30814 -> Server IPv4 UDP 30814

Reserve the server's LAN IP in the router or otherwise keep it stable.

VPS or dedicated hosting

Normally no home-router port forward is needed, but you still need to allow both TCP and UDP through the host firewall and any provider/cloud firewall.

8. Windows Firewall

Create inbound allow rules for:

TCP 30814
UDP 30814

You may also allow the BeamMP server executable itself. Do not rely on disabling Windows Firewall; explicit allow rules are preferable.

9. Linux firewall

For UFW:

sudo ufw allow 30814/tcp
sudo ufw allow 30814/udp
sudo ufw status

If your VPS provider has its own firewall/security group, configure the same TCP and UDP port there too.

10. Start and validate the vanilla server

Windows:

BeamMP-Server.exe

Linux:

cd /opt/beammp
sudo -u beammpserver ./BeamMP-Server

Review the console for [ERROR] and [WARN] lines before adding mods or plugins.

11. Joining the server

If BeamMP and the server are on the same PC, Direct Connect to:

127.0.0.1:30814

From another computer on the same LAN, use the server machine's LAN IPv4 and port.

For external private-server users, use the public IPv4 and configured port. For a public server with Private = false, users can normally find it in the BeamMP server list by the configured name.

12. Verify public connectivity

Run the server and use BeamMP's official connectivity checker with the public IPv4 and server port. Both TCP and UDP need to succeed.

If TCP works but UDP does not, check the UDP router rule, host firewall, provider firewall, and ServerConfig.toml port.

If home hosting cannot receive inbound connections even with correct rules, investigate whether the ISP uses CGNAT. In that case you may need a public IPv4, VPS, or game host.

13. Install client-distributed mods

BeamNG mods sent to joining clients belong in:

Resources/Client/

Example:

Resources/
└── Client/
    ├── race_truck.zip
    ├── buggy.zip
    └── support_vehicle.zip

Normal BeamNG mod ZIPs should generally remain zipped. If joining starts failing after new mods are added, remove the latest additions and retest.

14. Install a custom map

Put the map ZIP in:

Resources/Client/

Open the ZIP without extracting it and locate the map folder under levels/.

Example ZIP path:

levels/baja75_desert/

Then configure:

Map = "/levels/baja75_desert/info.json"

Restart and test.

15. BeamMP 3.x server-side plugin structure

Server-side Lua plugins belong under:

Resources/Server/

Each plugin needs its own folder, for example:

Resources/
└── Server/
    └── PlayerCounter/
        └── main.lua

Do not place the PlayerCounter main.lua directly in Resources/Server/.

BeamMP Server 3.x uses the current Lua 5.3 plugin system and explicit event registration.

16. Install PlayerCounter + Retention

Create:

Resources/Server/PlayerCounter/

Place the plugin at:

Resources/Server/PlayerCounter/main.lua

On first successful use, the analytics directory is:

Resources/Server/PlayerCounter/data/

with:

stats.json
joins.csv

17. Configure PlayerCounter

Open main.lua and set the analytics time zone.

UTC:

local UTC_OFFSET_HOURS = 0

Philippines / UTC+8:

local UTC_OFFSET_HOURS = 8

Choose the reporting time zone before collecting long-term data. Changing it later shifts calendar rollover boundaries.

Recommended guest behavior:

local COUNT_GUESTS_AS_UNIQUE = false

Guest sessions can still be counted without treating names as permanent identities.

Enable player chat commands:

local ALLOW_PLAYER_COMMANDS = true

Disable them:

local ALLOW_PLAYER_COMMANDS = false

18. PlayerCounter commands

Player chat commands, when enabled:

/playerstats
/retention

Server console commands:

playerstats
retention

playerstats reports daily, monthly, yearly, and all-time analytics. retention reports the current month-over-month retention cohort.

19. Metric definitions

A unique player is an authenticated BeamMP account identified by its permanent BeamMP ID.

If one player reconnects 15 times in one day:

Daily Unique Players: 1
Daily Join Sessions: 15

The plugin tracks daily, monthly, yearly, and all-time unique players and joins, plus new users, returning users, and retention.

A new player is a permanent BeamMP account ID not previously known to the database.

A returning player is an already-known player who participates again.

A retained player is a player from the immediately previous reporting cohort who returns in the current cohort.

Example:

July unique players:       400
July players back in Aug:  160
Monthly retention:        40.0%

20. Install the weekly webhook sender

Create:

Resources/Server/PlayerCounter/weekly_push.py

The recommended weekly sender performs these steps:

Read joins.csv.

Determine the most recently completed Monday-Sunday week.

Calculate weekly unique players.

Calculate weekly join sessions.

Calculate new players.

Calculate returning players.

Calculate previous-week retention.

Read current aggregate totals from stats.json.

Build JSON.

Sign the exact JSON body with HMAC-SHA256.

POST it to the website over HTTPS.

Keeping outbound HTTP outside BeamMP Lua avoids placing a network request inside BeamMP's synchronous Lua event execution path.

21. Recommended webhook payload

{
  "server": "Baja75 Series Server #4 NA",
  "period": {
    "start": "2026-08-03",
    "end": "2026-08-09"
  },
  "weekly": {
    "unique_players": 284,
    "join_sessions": 1328,
    "guest_joins": 14,
    "new_players": 72,
    "returning_players": 212,
    "retained_players": 146,
    "previous_week_players": 251,
    "retention_percent": 58.17
  },
  "current": {
    "month_unique": 381,
    "year_unique": 2184,
    "all_time_unique": 3719,
    "all_time_joins": 31527
  }
}

For a public website, send aggregate statistics rather than player names or BeamMP IDs.

22. Configure weekly_push.py

At minimum:

SERVER_NAME = "My BeamMP Server"
WEBHOOK_URL = "https://example.com/api/beammp-weekly"
WEBHOOK_SECRET = "CHANGE_THIS_TO_A_LONG_RANDOM_SECRET"
UTC_OFFSET_HOURS = 0

Use the same time-zone offset as PlayerCounter.

For Philippines reporting:

UTC_OFFSET_HOURS = 8

23. Webhook security

Use HTTPS and a strong private secret. Do not expose the secret in GitHub, client-side JavaScript, public HTML, screenshots, or Discord.

Recommended signing method:

HMAC-SHA256

Recommended header:

X-BeamMP-Signature: <signature>

The website should calculate HMAC-SHA256 over the exact raw request body using its private copy of the same secret. Save the payload only when the signatures match.

Typical HTTP responses:

200 OK            accepted
400 Bad Request   malformed payload
401 Unauthorized  bad signature
409 Conflict      duplicate week, if duplicates are rejected

24. Test the webhook manually

Linux:

cd /opt/beammp
sudo -u beammpserver \
  python3 Resources/Server/PlayerCounter/weekly_push.py

Windows Command Prompt:

cd C:\BeamMP-Server
python Resources\Server\PlayerCounter\weekly_push.py

PowerShell:

Set-Location C:\BeamMP-Server
python .\Resources\Server\PlayerCounter\weekly_push.py

Verify that the JSON looks correct, the week dates are right, the site returns HTTP 200, and the website stores the expected record before scheduling automatic delivery.

25. Linux: run BeamMP with systemd

Create:

/etc/systemd/system/beammp.service

[Unit]
Description=BeamMP Server
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=beammpserver
Group=beammpserver
WorkingDirectory=/opt/beammp
ExecStart=/opt/beammp/BeamMP-Server
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
NoNewPrivileges=true

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

Enable and start:

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable beammp.service
sudo systemctl start beammp.service

Useful commands:

systemctl status beammp.service
sudo systemctl restart beammp.service
sudo systemctl stop beammp.service
journalctl -u beammp.service
journalctl -u beammp.service -f

26. Linux: weekly stats scheduler

Create:

/etc/systemd/system/beammp-weekly-stats.service

[Unit]
Description=Push BeamMP Weekly Player Statistics
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=beammpserver
Group=beammpserver
WorkingDirectory=/opt/beammp
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /opt/beammp/Resources/Server/PlayerCounter/weekly_push.py

Create:

/etc/systemd/system/beammp-weekly-stats.timer

[Unit]
Description=BeamMP Weekly Statistics Timer

[Timer]
OnCalendar=Mon *-*-* 00:10:00
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target

Enable it:

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now beammp-weekly-stats.timer

Check it:

systemctl status beammp-weekly-stats.timer
systemctl list-timers beammp-weekly-stats.timer

Test the push immediately:

sudo systemctl start beammp-weekly-stats.service
journalctl -u beammp-weekly-stats.service

Persistent=true lets systemd account for a missed calendar activation after downtime.

